# Postgres connection string, used by the running app. Locally, run `npx prisma dev` and
# copy the printed TCP url here. In production use the POOLED Neon string — the host with
# `-pooler` in it. Every function instance opens its own pool (see src/lib/prisma.ts), so
# what the database sees is instances × pool size, and the shared pooler is what keeps
# that under Postgres's connection limit.
DATABASE_URL=""

# The same database WITHOUT the pooler, used only by the Prisma CLI: migrate, db push,
# db seed. Leave it unset locally, where DATABASE_URL is already a direct connection.
#
# It is separate because the pooler cannot run migrations. Neon's pooled endpoint is
# PgBouncer in transaction mode, and Prisma Migrate needs a session — it takes an advisory
# lock for the migration lock and issues DDL that must outlive a single statement. Pointed
# at the pooled host, `migrate deploy` hangs or fails rather than doing the wrong thing
# quietly, but it still costs a deploy to find out.
#
# prisma.config.ts prefers this and falls back to DATABASE_URL, so an unset value is the
# correct local setting rather than a missing one.
DIRECT_URL=""

# Auth.js — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=""

# Google sign-in. From console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials →
# Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application.
#
# The authorised redirect URI must be exactly:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
# and the same path on your production domain. A mismatch here is the single most common
# cause of redirect_uri_mismatch at the consent screen.
#
# Auth.js v5 picks these two names up on its own — they are not read anywhere in the
# source, so renaming them silently disables Google sign-in.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

# Google Gemini (free tier) — get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=""
GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-flash-latest"

# Max Gemini evaluations one user may run per UTC day. Set to "0" to switch
# evaluations off entirely. Defaults to 5 if unset or not a whole number.
DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT="5"

# Max evaluations across ALL users per UTC day. Commented out = no ceiling.
#
# This is the one that actually protects a shared free-tier key: the per-user limit above
# only governs how fast one account burns through, and sign-in is open to anyone with a
# Google account. Set it before deploying publicly.
#
# Keep it at or under HALF the free-tier requests-per-day published for GEMINI_MODEL:
# this app counts UTC days and Google's quota resets on its own schedule, so the two
# windows overlap and a ceiling of N can allow close to 2N inside one of Google's days.
#
# Look the number up for YOUR model and key rather than assuming it is generous — the
# default here, gemini-flash-latest, currently resolves to gemini-3.6-flash, whose free
# tier serves 20 requests per day. Half of that is the 10 suggested below. Set this too
# high and it never fires: Google refuses instead, and a learner is told to retry in
# minutes for an allowance that does not return until tomorrow.
#
# Left commented rather than empty so that copying this file cannot be mistaken for
# configuring a ceiling of zero, which would refuse every submission.
# GLOBAL_DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT="10"

# Max Gemini calls per minute across all users. Commented out = no rate gate.
#
# The daily ceiling bounds the total; this bounds the burst. Free-tier quotas are
# per-minute as well as per-day, and simultaneous submissions become simultaneous API
# calls. A request refused here costs the user nothing — no daily slot is charged.
# GEMINI_RPM_LIMIT="5"

# Evaluations per UTC day shared by ALL accounts younger than 24 hours. Commented out =
# new accounts are not restricted.
#
# The per-user limit assumes accounts cost something to make. Google sign-in is what
# makes that true — minting accounts now means minting Google accounts, which Google
# gates with phone verification at scale. This is the defence in depth behind it: a
# farmed account is always a new account, so however many are created they share this one
# allowance and the rest of the ceiling stays for people who were here yesterday.
#
# Enforced only once the day's total passes half the ceiling above. Below that line
# nothing is being contended, so refusing a newcomer would waste quota and lose a real
# person for no gain; above it, this is what keeps a flood of fresh accounts from
# crowding out everyone who was already here.
#
# It does not stop patience. Accounts registered today are established tomorrow, which is
# inherent to any age-based rule — the aim is that farming stops paying off inside a
# session. "0" stops new accounts evaluating at all until they age in.
# NEW_ACCOUNT_DAILY_LIMIT="20"

# What ONE account may evaluate on its first day, in place of DAILY_EVAL_LIMIT above.
# Commented out = new accounts get the ordinary quota immediately.
#
# The allowance above is first-come-first-served, so without this a handful of accounts
# drink all of it: at five each, four accounts empty a pool of twenty before anyone in
# another timezone is awake. Capping the individual spreads the same pool across more
# people and makes draining it cost more accounts.
# FIRST_DAY_EVAL_LIMIT="2"
